Wampanoag,Maushop,Massachusetts (Cape Cod / Martha's Vineyard / Nantucket),Giant culture-hero,"Immense size (a true giant); associated with physically shaping the landscape (said to have created Cape Cod, Martha's Vineyard, and Nantucket); left giant footprint-like impressions in the landscape; a stretch of Massachusetts beach is named after him (Moshup Beach).",None substantiated in sourced material beyond his origin as a giant; framed as a benevolent culture-hero rather than a hidden or feared entity.,"Maushop (also spelled Moshup) is one of the most prominent figures in Wampanoag legend, a benevolent giant credited with creating the islands of Cape Cod, Martha's Vineyard (Noepe), and Nantucket. One version of his story holds that he created Martha's Vineyard to escape violence on the mainland; when homesick, he attempted to build a bridge back by scattering huge boulders into the water. Unlike many wildman figures, Maushop is remembered as a folk hero, not a hidden or feared creature -- he is honored rather than avoided.",https://emjwriter.com/2016/11/16/weird-history-part-eight-stories-from-wampanoag-culture-that-you-can-share-at-thanksgiving/,1.5
Wampanoag,Pukwudgie,"Massachusetts (centered on Hockomock Swamp, Bridgewater Triangle region)",Small trickster spirit,"Small stature, roughly 2-3 feet tall; grey skin, sometimes described as glowing; oversized features (nose, ears, fingers); able to transform into a porcupine in some tellings.","Can lead travelers astray, cloud memory, lure people with mysterious lights; some stories say they persuade people to leap from cliffs; attack with short knives or poisoned arrows; associated with fire manipulation and shapeshifting.","Pukwudgies are small, trickster woodland spirits in Wampanoag tradition (and related traditions among other northeastern Algonquian peoples), whose legends center on Hockomock Swamp -- Algonquian for 'place where spirits dwell' -- the largest freshwater swamp in Massachusetts. Their origin is tied to jealousy of the giant Maushop. They are morally mixed: capable of helping or teaching, but also genuinely dangerous when angered or disrespected. Their legends persist today in the folklore of the Bridgewater Triangle, a name coined by cryptozoologist Loren Coleman in the 1970s -- worth noting that some modern 'strange creature' reports tied to this same swamp reflect that 20th-century paranormal-enthusiast tradition layered on top of the older Wampanoag material, not the old material itself. A separate Wampanoag term, Nikommo, also appears in some source lists for 'little people' and has not yet been researched in depth -- open thread.",https://newenglandhistoricalsociety.com/the-pukwudgies-of-the-bridgewater-triangle/,1.5
Cherokee,Tsul 'Kalu (also known as Judaculla),Appalachian Mountains (North Carolina / Tennessee / Western Carolina highlands),Giant wildman / guardian hunter,"Described as a giant with long, tangled hair; glowing/reddish eyes; enormous physical strength and powerful limbs; keen eyesight; leaves large footprint-like impressions in soil and rock along mountain ridges -- a specific rock formation (Judaculla Rock) is pointed to as physical evidence; dwells far from villages in remote high mountain country; recorded by Europeans as early as 1823 (one of the oldest continuously documented entities in this database).","Said to be able to read people's thoughts and control minds at will; described as able to leap across entire mountains in a single motion; dwells in a specific named place, Tsunegun'yi.","Tsul 'Kalu (""he has them slanting/sloping"", referring to his eyes), also called Judaculla or the Cherokee Devil, is a giant, hairy figure of Cherokee mythology who plays the role of the great lord of the hunt, invoked in hunting rites and rituals. He is generally respected rather than feared -- a protector of the wilderness and a guardian of respectful, responsible hunting -- though accounts describing him as vengeful toward those who disrespect him also exist (one version holds he became hostile after being called 'ugly' in the Cherokee language). Multiple independent modern sources explicitly connect Tsul 'Kalu to Bigfoot folklore directly, not merely by outside inference -- he is one of the most frequently cited Indigenous parallels to Bigfoot in cryptozoology literature. In the plural form, Tsul 'Kalu is also used as the name for a traditional race of giants said to dwell farther west.",https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsul_'Kalu,1.5
